<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiananmen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1420">1420</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Gate of Heavenly Peace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiananmen, the Gate of Heavenly Peace, is the monumental gate at the southern entrance to the Imperial City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, first built in 1420 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> and rebuilt in 1651. From its rostrum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> on 1 October 1949, and with his portrait it became a national emblem, overlooking Tiananmen Square.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiananmen.jpg" alt="Tiananmen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Balon Greyjoy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20200110_Tiananmen-3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chinese name, Tiananmen, is made of the characters for heaven, peace and gate, which is why it is conventionally translated as the Gate of Heavenly Peace. That rendering is somewhat misleading, since the name derives from a much longer phrase meaning &quot;receiving the mandate from heaven, and pacifying the dynasty&quot;. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> name, Abkai elhe obure duka, lies closer to it and can be rendered the Gate of Heavenly Peacemaking.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> the gate was called Chengtianmen, the Gate of Accepting Heavenly Mandate, and was modelled on a gate of the same name at an imperial building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. Lightning burned it down in July 1457. In 1465 the Chenghua Emperor ordered the Minister of Works, Zigui, to rebuild it, and the design changed from a free-standing paifang to the gatehouse seen today.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The building is 66 metres long, 37 metres wide and 32 metres high, and carries roof decorations reserved for imperial buildings. Two lions stand in front of the gate and two more guard the bridges, since lions are believed in Chinese culture to protect people from evil spirits. Two stone columns called huabiao, each topped by an animal, also stand before it. Such columns were first designed for commoners to post petitions, but those in front of the Imperial City were purely decorative and signified the majesty of the imperial government. The reviewing stands in the foreground are used on International Workers' Day (1 May) and National Day (1 October).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The gate was burned again in 1644 by rebels led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> conquest it was rebuilt from 1645 and received its present name on completion in 1651. Between 1969 and 1970 it was reconstructed once more, because it had deteriorated partly through heavy use in the 1950s and 1960s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> ordered the work kept secret, and it was officially called a renovation.</p><p>Its position at the front of the Imperial City gave it political weight. A portrait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> hung there in 1925 and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> in 1945. On 7 July 1949 portraits of Zhu De and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> were hung, and from 1 October 1949 a single portrait of Mao has been displayed, replaced each year before National Day. Only once, on 9 March 1953, was it temporarily replaced, by a portrait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> on the occasion of his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiananmen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference adopted a Common Program in September, and on 1 October 1949 Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic from the <b>Gate of Heavenly Peace</b> in Beijing, which again became the capital, while the Nationalist government withdrew to Taiwan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On the afternoon of May 4 over 4,000 students of Yenching University, Peking University and other schools gathered in front of <b>Tiananmen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The palace's central north-south axis became the central axis of Beijing, running south through <b>Tiananmen</b> Gate towards Yongdingmen and north through Jingshan Park to the Drum Tower and Bell Tower.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1926 officers of the Whampoa Military Academy set to it the patriotic song &quot;Revolution of the Citizens&quot;, associated with the First United Front between the Kuomintang and the Chinese Communist Party, and demonstrators in <b>Tiananmen</b> Square chanted political slogans to it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques</a></li><li><blockquote>In late September 1950, on a work trip to Beijing, he passed through <b>Tiananmen</b> Square as people prepared for National Day on 1 October, the first anniversary of the People's Republic, and was moved by the festive sea of flags; he wrote a first draft on the train…</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland</a></li><li><blockquote>…24 October 1976, at a rally celebrating the fall of the Gang of Four, the new party chairman Hua Guofeng led the other leaders onto the <b>Tiananmen</b> rostrum to the song, which for a short while served to praise him and was used in newsreels and documentary films.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
